  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 202 PM MDT Mon Jun 15 2026\n\nLow pressure system spinning near the west side of the Hudson Bay\nis our main weather maker for the short term forecast. It's \ngiving the CWA northwest flow with just enough upward vertical \nmotion to get some showers and thunderstorms going in the upper \ngreat plains. The storms so far have been mostly contained to the \nnorth-central and eastern half of SD while the far western \ncounties have been dry for the most part, save for a few cells \nthat have been short-lived.\n\nLooking ahead to tonight and tomorrow, continued northwest flow \nwill keep the chances for isolated showers and non-severe \nthunderstorms to track into the area from the north. These chances\nwill be mainly centered around little shortwaves spinning around \nthe main low that will keep spinning near Hudson Bay. Temps \ntonight will dip into the 40s to low 50s and tomorrow's highs will\nbe above normal, in the 80s to low 90s. Winds will be out of the \nwest-to-northwest at up to 10 to 20 mph in the afternoon, a little\nstronger in the NW SD plains where sustained speeds could be up \nto 25 mph, before calming down in the evening.\n\nWednesday and Thursday look similar to tomorrow in terms of the \nPoPs forecast, continued chances for isolated showers and storms \nin the northwest counties. There will be a couple differences \nthough. For starters, afternoon temps will be a little cooler, \nonly reaching the 70s for the plains and only the 60s to low 70s \nfor the Black Hills. The other difference will be the winds on \nWednesday. Strong northwest winds at 15 to 30 mph and gusts \nnearing 45 to 55 are likely Wednesday afternoon. Strongest winds \nwill be in the SD plains. Thursday will be a calmer with northwest\nwinds around 10 to 20 mph and gusts near 30 mph.\n\nFriday and the weekend will see the weather pattern change to a \nmore westerly flow. This will bring back more widespread \nthunderstorm chances for the weekend and warmer temps.",
